<header class="page-hero wrap">
  <span class="eyebrow">Tramontina</span>
  <h1>Nesting Mixing Bowls</h1>
  <p class="lead">A nesting mixing-bowl family with soft-touch lids — taken from market research and shape studies through five nesting-geometry studies in CAD, to prototypes finished and mold-cast by hand in the shop.</p>
</header>

<article class="case">

  <figure style="margin-top:0"><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/final-family.jpg" alt="The Nesting Mixing Bowls family — mixing bowls and prep bowls with lids, rendered as a set" /></figure>

  <div class="case-meta">
    <div>Product design, market research, CAD modeling, rendering, 3D printing, mold making &amp; casting</div>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"><h2><span class="num">01</span>Research — what makes a mixing bowl iconic</h2></div>

  <p>Before sketching anything, the market got mapped. Six brands — KitchenAid, Williams-Sonoma, All-Clad, Cuisinart, OXO, and Norpro — were surveyed for the capacities they actually sell, establishing where the common sizes cluster and where a new set had to land. In parallel, a silhouette survey broke the category's shapes down to their key characteristics: the rounded raised band, the base, the curved lip.</p>

  <div class="gal">
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/research-sizes.jpg" alt="Mixing bowl average sizes across six brands — donut chart from 1 quart to 5 quarts" loading="lazy" /></figure>
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/research-shapes.jpg" alt="Bowl silhouettes survey with key characteristics — rounded raised band, base, curved lip" loading="lazy" /></figure>
  </div>
  <figcaption style="font-size:13px;color:var(--on-dark-muted);margin-top:10px;font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic">Market survey — capacity clusters across six competitor brands, and the silhouette language of the category.</figcaption>

  <p>A mixing bowl is a tool you use <em>with</em> other tools. Interaction studies looked at how a whisk and a hand mixer actually meet the bowl — upright for beating, tilted for folding — geometry that decides wall angle, depth, and how the bowl behaves when it's held at a slant.</p>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/research-tools.jpg" alt="Tool interaction studies — whisk and hand mixer shown upright and tilted in bowl sections" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Bowls in use — whisk and hand-mixer geometry, upright and tilted, informing wall angle and depth.</figcaption></figure>

  <div class="divider"><h2><span class="num">02</span>Form development</h2></div>

  <p>Sketch explorations ran in tagged threads pinned to the wall — a <b>ring</b> concept, a <b>stub</b> handle direction, <b>lid</b> studies, and base treatments — worked over the shape survey and size data from research.</p>

  <div class="gal">
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/sketch-wall-survey.jpg" alt="Sketch wall — shape survey and iconic shape studies pinned above concept sketches" loading="lazy" /></figure>
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/sketch-wall-ring.jpg" alt="Sketch wall — ring and stub concept threads with tagged sticky notes" loading="lazy" /></figure>
  </div>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/sketch-wall-lids.jpg" alt="Sketch wall — lid and bottom concept sketches" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Concept threads tagged on the wall — ring, stub, lids, bottoms — each a different answer to grip, pour, and storage.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>The sketches converged on a family of soft, continuous silhouettes with a pour lip and a non-slip base — and early CAD concepts tested variations on the theme, including a version with a metal clip detail at the rim.</p>

  <div class="gal">
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/concept-silhouettes.jpg" alt="Refined concept silhouettes — four bowl profiles with pour spout and non-slip base" loading="lazy" /></figure>
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/early-set.jpg" alt="Early CAD concept — nested set with red lids" loading="lazy" /></figure>
  </div>
  <p class="ph">One or two sentences on how these directions were evaluated and what decided the direction that moved forward — e.g. feedback from the team at Tramontina, manufacturing constraints, or cost.</p>

  <div class="divider"><h2><span class="num">03</span>Nesting geometry</h2></div>

  <p>In a nesting set, the section <em>is</em> the product. Five candidate profiles were modeled and cut open in CAD — each studied for how tightly the four bowls seat into one another, how the stack height grows, and where the lids live when the set is put away.</p>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/nesting-studies.jpg" alt="Four of five nesting geometry studies — CAD section views of the nested set" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Section studies — candidate nesting profiles compared for seat, stack height, and wall consistency.</figcaption></figure>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/stack-drawing.jpg" alt="1:1 scale line drawing of the nested stack" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The nested stack drafted at 1:1 — the profile that carried forward.</figcaption></figure>

  <div class="divider"><h2><span class="num">04</span>The resolved design</h2></div>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/final-single.jpg" alt="The resolved bowl — deep red body with a soft-touch black lid" loading="lazy" /></figure>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Soft-touch lid</b> — a flexible overmolded lid with a wide, grippable rim seals each bowl for prep-ahead and storage.</li>
    <li><b>Bowls seat on their lids</b> — flipped under the bowl, the lid becomes a non-slip base, keeping the bowl planted on the counter while mixing.</li>
    <li><b>A family, not just a set</b> — the nesting logic scales from full mixing bowls down to small prep bowls, all sharing the same silhouette.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="ph">Confirm/adjust the feature list above — especially the lid-as-base behavior and intended materials (e.g. melamine/ABS body, TPE lid) — and add any features I couldn't see in the renders.</p>

  <div class="gal">
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/final-nested.jpg" alt="Cutaway render of the nested set" loading="lazy" /></figure>
    <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/final-stacked.jpg" alt="The set stacked with lids on" loading="lazy" /></figure>
