  </div>
  <p class="ph">Caption or short paragraph on the scooped-rim detail (grip? pour?) and whether colorways were part of the proposal.</p>

  <div class="divider"><h2><span class="num">05</span>Prototyping — making it real</h2></div>

  <p>The design didn't stay on screen. A full-scale bowl was 3D printed, then sanded, primed, and sprayed to a production-level gloss — the same hand-finishing discipline as the Kitchen Shears prototypes, at several times the size.</p>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/proto-bowl.jpg" alt="Bowl prototype sequence — 3D print sanded, primed, spray-painted, and held finished" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Print → sand → prime → paint — a full-scale bowl finished by hand to a production-level surface.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>The lid needed to <em>feel</em> right, not just look right — so it was cast, not printed. Shop-built acrylic mold boxes, vacuum-degassed silicone, and two-part molds produced flexible lid prototypes that could actually be stretched, seated, and peeled like the real thing.</p>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/proto-molds.jpg" alt="Mold-making sequence — acrylic mold box, vacuum degassing, cured two-part silicone molds, cast lid" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Mold making — acrylic boxes, degassing under vacuum, two-part silicone molds, and the first cast pulls.</figcaption></figure>

  <figure><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/proto-lid-tests.jpg" alt="Cast lid prototypes sectioned for finish tests — matte and gloss halves compared" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Finish tests — cast lids sectioned and compared, matte against gloss, to land the right surface.</figcaption></figure>

  <figure style="margin-top:40px"><img src="/assets/img/mixing-bowls/final-on-lids-wide.jpg" alt="Two bowls seated on their inverted lids, one upright and one tilted" loading="lazy" /></figure>

